## III Direct Input-Output Training Method for Control

In this section, we train several networks to replicate the behavior of the controller we designed. Inputs to the network are 𝒙¯\bar{\bm{x}} as illustrated by ([3](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.08714v1#S2.E3 "In II Sliding Mode Controller Design ‣ Hybrid Neural Network and Conventional Controller Approach for Robust Control of Highly Unstable Systems: Application to Tilt-Rotor Control")) and its desired vector 𝒙¯d\bar{\bm{x}}\_{d} for each time step. The outputs of the network are the control signals for the next step, denoted by 𝒖\bm{u} as represented in ([4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.08714v1#S2.E4 "In II Sliding Mode Controller Design ‣ Hybrid Neural Network and Conventional Controller Approach for Robust Control of Highly Unstable Systems: Application to Tilt-Rotor Control")). Given the multi-level control allocation required for a conventional controller, the direct input-output control method may offer the advantage of reduced computational cost.

