𝒙¯¨^6×1=𝒂^6×1+𝑮^6×8​𝒗8×1,\hat{{\ddot{\bar{\bm{x}}}}}_{6\times 1}=\bm{\hat{a}}_{6\times 1}+\bm{\hat{G}}_{6\times 8}\bm{v}_{8\times 1},

where 𝒂^\bm{\hat{a}} and 𝑮^\bm{\hat{G}} are the approximations of 𝒂\bm{a} and 𝑮\bm{G}, respectively. 𝑮\bm{G} is a matrix that relates the arrangement of the actuators to the dynamics. Thus, it is evident that 𝑮\bm{G} varies with the attitude angles ϕ\phi, θ\theta, and ψ\psi considering the fixed physical parameters of the plant-[III](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.08714v1#S3.T3 "TABLE III ‣ III-E Implementation on the Dynamic System ‣ III Direct Input-Output Training Method for Control ‣ Hybrid Neural Network and Conventional Controller Approach for Robust Control of Highly Unstable Systems: Application to Tilt-Rotor Control"). Since XX, YY, and ZZ are position states expressed in the inertia frame, it is clear that 𝒂\bm{a} varies with ϕ\phi, ϕ˙\dot{\phi}, θ\theta, θ˙\dot{\theta}, ψ\psi, and ψ˙\dot{\psi}. Therefore, we need to predict 48 items for 𝑮^\bm{\hat{G}} based on 3 inputs and 6 items for 𝒂^\bm{\hat{a}} based on 6 inputs. We use the dataset represented in [I](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.08714v1#S3.T1 "TABLE I ‣ III-A Data Collection ‣ III Direct Input-Output Training Method for Control ‣ Hybrid Neural Network and Conventional Controller Approach for Robust Control of Highly Unstable Systems: Application to Tilt-Rotor Control"), but with half the number of data points for training.

