### IV-B Baseline System Simulation

The approximations of plant dynamics parameters, 𝒂^\bm{\hat{a}} and 𝑮^\bm{\hat{G}}, are predicted using neural networks based on necessary feedback in each loop and incorporated into the SMC controller designed in Section [II](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.08714v1#S2 "II Sliding Mode Controller Design ‣ Hybrid Neural Network and Conventional Controller Approach for Robust Control of Highly Unstable Systems: Application to Tilt-Rotor Control"). The controller uses the predicted 𝒂^\bm{\hat{a}} and 𝑮^\bm{\hat{G}} in place of the unknown plant parameters 𝒂\bm{a} and 𝑮\bm{G} in Equation ([11](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.08714v1#S2.E11 "In II Sliding Mode Controller Design ‣ Hybrid Neural Network and Conventional Controller Approach for Robust Control of Highly Unstable Systems: Application to Tilt-Rotor Control")). For the simulation, the controller is applied to the exact plant dynamics. The implementation results on test trajectory, using MLP and LSTM plant dynamics predictors are visualized in Fig. [8](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.08714v1#S4.F8 "Fig. 8 ‣ IV-B Baseline System Simulation ‣ IV A Neural Network- Based Sliding Mode Control ‣ Hybrid Neural Network and Conventional Controller Approach for Robust Control of Highly Unstable Systems: Application to Tilt-Rotor Control") and Fig. [9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2606.08714v1#S4.F9 "Fig. 9 ‣ IV-B Baseline System Simulation ‣ IV A Neural Network- Based Sliding Mode Control ‣ Hybrid Neural Network and Conventional Controller Approach for Robust Control of Highly Unstable Systems: Application to Tilt-Rotor Control").

